    "case_id": "535",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old woman with locally advanced, unresectable pancreatic adenocarcinoma. She initially received four cycles of Folfirinox chemotherapy without significant response, followed by gemcitabine and nab-paclitaxel therapy. After 20 cycles of combined therapy and 16 additional cycles of nab-paclitaxel alone, she developed bilateral facial drooping, inability to close her eyelids, and difficulty raising her eyebrows. Prior to this, she had experienced grade 1 peripheral neuropathy which remained stable while taking vitamin B6 supplements, and had developed severe edema due to capillary leak which led to discontinuation of gemcitabine.\n\nNeurological evaluation revealed bilateral lower motor neuron facial muscle weakness.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  * Lyme titers: Normal\n  * Creatine kinase: Normal\n  * Rapid plasma reagin: Normal\n  * Antinuclear antibodies: Normal\n  * Rheumatoid factor: Normal\n  * Sjögren antibody: Normal\n  * Serum angiotensin-converting enzyme levels: Normal\n\n- Imaging Studies:\n  * MRI Brain\n    Title: Brain MRI with contrast\n    Description: Revealed bilateral facial nerve enhancement compatible with bilateral facial neuritis, without evidence of intracranial or leptomeningeal malignancy",
